        <section id="overview" class="intro">
          <div class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-line"></span>Orientation</div>
          <h1>Prepare the tools,<br /><em>then observe the system.</em></h1>
          <p class="lead">This shared sequence prepares every team to work in a cloned GitHub repository using Visual Studio Code, Arduino CLI, Arduino IDE, and Processing before running the physical robot and Digital Twin examples.</p>
        </section>
        <section class="orientation-card">
          <div class="orientation-icon">→</div>
          <div><span class="section-kicker">Jumping in the Deep End</span><h2>It's OK to feel a little uncomfortable.</h2><p>On the first day of a new job, a lot of your time will be spent getting familiar with new tools, processes and materials.  This experience is intended to be the same.  You can take your time and ask questions.</p>
</div>
        </section>
        </section>
        <section class="orientation-card">
          <div class="orientation-icon">→</div>
          <div><span class="section-kicker">Help & Support</span><h2>The following steps will be followed in the first teaching session.</h2><p>The first teaching session will be used to help you set up all the tools and processes you need to make progress with the course.</p>
</div>
        </section>
        <div class="sequence-actions" aria-label="Exercise section controls"><button type="button" data-exercise-action="collapse">Collapse all</button><button type="button" data-exercise-action="open">Open all</button></div>
        <div class="exercise-sequence">
          <article id="exercise-01" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">01</div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading"><div><span class="concept-tag">Hardware orientation · robot and controller</span><h2>Meet the 3Pi+ and StampC3</h2></div>
<span class="time-badge">10–15 min</span></div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <figure class="orientation-figure"><img src="assets/images/pololu3pi_stampc3/Pololu3Pi_StampC3.png" alt="Top view of the Pololu 3Pi+ robot with the StampC3 controller mounted at its centre" width="100%" /></figure>
                <p>Your physical system combines a <a href="https://www.pololu.com/docs/0J83">Pololu 3Pi+ 32U4 mobile robot</a> with an <a href="https://docs.m5stack.com/en/core/stamp_c3">M5Stack StampC3 controller</a>. Inspect the system to identify the parts that sense, move, and communicate. You can ask a member of teaching staff about the parts of your robot.</p>
                <p>For this coursework, you will program the <strong>StampC3 controller</strong>, shown at the centre of the robot and labelled below.</p>
                <figure class="orientation-figure"><img src="assets/images/pololu3pi_stampc3/Pololu3Pi_StampC3_Annotated_Simple.png" alt="Annotated top view of the robot labelling the StampC3, reset button, on-off button, push-button input, and USB-C programming port" width="100%" /></figure>
                <p>The Pololu 3Pi+ runs supplied middleware that operates its motors and sensors. The StampC3 requests sensor information and sends motor commands.</p>
                <p>We will learn more about the robot and the Digital Twin through these exercise pages. Before using it, set up the software tools and verify that the supplied system works. <strong>It is good practice to confirm that something works before modifying it.</strong></p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-02" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">02</div>
            <div class="exercise-content"><div class="exercise-heading"><div><span class="concept-tag">Development environment · hardware</span><h2>Install and Configure the Arduino IDE, Arduino CLI</h2></div>
<span class="time-badge">25–35 min</span></div>
<div class="teaching-brief"><p>We will use the Arduino framework to programme the StampC3.  We will install both the Arduino IDE (integrated development environment) and the Arduino CLI (command line interface).  </p>
</div>
<div class="exercise-grid"><div class="investigation"><h3>Setup checklist</h3><ol><li>Select and install <a href="https://www.arduino.cc/en/software" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Arduino IDE</a>. </li><li>In Arduino IDE, navigate to Preferences and add M5Stack's Board Manager URL: <code>https://static-cdn.m5stack.com/resource/arduino/package_m5stack_index.json</code>.  You will need to copy and paste this URL into the board manager text box, as shown below.</li>
<p align="center"><img src="./assets/images/setup/arduino_preferences.png" alt="Screenshot of the preferences panel in Arduino IDE" width="100%"></p> 
<li>Install the M5Stack board package: select Tools → Board Manager.  Type "StampC3" into the text entry field, and wait for the list to update. Select the latest version, and then click "Install".</li>
<p align="center"><img src="./assets/images/setup/arduino_boards_manager.png" alt="Screenshot of the boards manager panel in Arduino IDE" width="100%"></p>
<li>Select the correct board to program: select Tools → Board → M5Stack Arduino → M5StackC3. </li>
<li>Configure boot options: select and set <strong>Tools → USB-CDC On Boot → Disabled</strong>.</li>
<li>You can now close the Arduino IDE</li>
<li>Install <a href="https://docs.arduino.cc/arduino-cli/installation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Arduino CLI</a> and confirm that <code>arduino-cli version</code> works in a new terminal.</li></ol><div class="measure-row"><span><b>Check</b> M5StampC3 is selected</span><span><b>Check</b> Arduino CLI runs</span><span><b>Keep</b> setup errors</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-03" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">03</div>
            <div class="exercise-content"><div class="exercise-heading"><div><span class="concept-tag">Shared code · account access</span><h2>Setup GitHub Access</h2></div>
<span class="time-badge">10–15 min</span></div>
<div class="teaching-brief"><p>Your team repository is the shared workspace for code, measurements, analysis, checkpoints, and reports. GitHub is an industry tool that allows for different versions of work to be maintained and developed by teams.  You will need to setup a GitHub account.  In the first teaching session, this task may be completed ahead of this exercise.</p>
            </div>
<div class="exercise-grid">
            <div class="investigation"><h3>Access checklist</h3><ol><li>Create a GitHub account using your university email address.</li><li>Send your GitHub username to teaching staff.   and accept the invitation to your pre-populated team repository.</li><li>Confirm you can view the repository in a browser and identify its clone address.</li></ol><div class="measure-row"><span><b>Check</b> team repository access</span><span><b>Know</b> your GitHub username</span><span><b>Keep</b> the clone address</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-04" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">04</div>
            <div class="exercise-content"><div class="exercise-heading"><div><span class="concept-tag">Development environment · source control</span><h2>Install and Configure VS Code</h2></div>
<span class="time-badge">15–25 min</span></div>
<div class="teaching-brief"><p>VS Code is the recommended editor for this coursework. It provides the editor, integrated terminal, Git interface, and access to the supplied Arduino CLI tasks.</p>
</div>
<div class="exercise-grid"><div class="investigation"><h3>Setup checklist</h3><ol><li>Install <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/download" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visual Studio Code</a> and <a href="https://git-scm.com/downloads" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Git</a>.</li><li>Install the <strong>C/C++</strong> extension from the Extensions view. Click the small extensions icon on the left panel, select "C/C++ Intellisense, debugging and code browsing", and click install.</li>
<p align="center"><img src="./assets/images/setup/vs_code_extensions_panel.png" alt="Screenshot of the extensions panel in VS Code" width="100%"></p>
<li>Open a terminal, type into the terminal the following command: <code>git --version</code>.  If you see text like <code>git version 2.43.0</code> you have Git installed correctly.</li>
<p align="center"><img src="./assets/images/setup/vs_code_new_terminal.png" alt="Screenshot opening a new terminal in VS Code" width="100%"></p>

</ol><div class="measure-row"><span><b>Check</b> VS Code opens</span><span><b>Check</b> Git runs</span><span><b>Install</b> C/C++ extension</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-05" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">05</div>
            <div class="exercise-content"><div class="exercise-heading"><div><span class="concept-tag">Shared code · local workspace</span><h2>Clone the Team Repository</h2></div>
<span class="time-badge">10–15 min</span></div>
<div class="teaching-brief"><p>Cloning makes a complete local working copy of your team's supplied repository. This is where you will edit code, run tasks, record evidence, and later commit shared work.</p>
</div>
<div class="exercise-grid"><div class="investigation"><h3>Clone checklist</h3>
<ol>
<li>In VS Code, select <strong>Source Control → Clone Repository</strong> and sign in to GitHub if prompted.</li>
<p align="center"><img src="./assets/images/setup/vs_code_source_control.png" alt="Screenshot of opening the source control panel in VS Code" width="100%"></p>
<li>Select your team repository and choose a location on your computer where you can keep the workspace.</li>
<li>Open the cloned folder in VS Code and choose <strong>Trust</strong>.</li>
<p align="center"><img src="./assets/images/setup/vs_code_trust.png" alt="Screenshot of selecting Trust in VS Code" width="100%"></p>
<li>Confirm the Explorer contains <strong>StampC3_Template</strong>, <strong>DigitalTwin</strong>, <strong>investigations</strong>, and <strong>.vscode</strong>.</li></ol><div class="measure-row"><span><b>Clone</b> the team repository</span><span><b>Open</b> its root folder</span><span><b>Check</b> supplied folders</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-06" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">06</div>
            <div class="exercise-content"><div class="exercise-heading"><div><span class="concept-tag">Individual Phase 1 work · recoverable code</span><h2>Prepare Your Individual Workspace</h2></div>
<span class="time-badge">10–15 min</span></div>
<div class="teaching-brief"><p>Prepare a personal working copy of the StampC3 template inside the code folder for your allocated Phase 1 specialism. The supplied top-level template remains unchanged as a recovery point.</p>
</div>
<div class="exercise-grid"><div class="investigation"><h3>Workspace checklist</h3><ol><li>Open your allocated folder under <code>investigations/phase-1/</code>, for example <code>student-1-motors</code>, then open its <code>code/</code> directory.</li><li>Copy the complete top-level <strong>StampC3_Template</strong> folder into that <code>code/</code> directory.</li><li>Rename the copied folder and its main <strong>.ino</strong> file to the same meaningful progress name, such as <strong>Test1</strong>.</li><li>Confirm your copied sketch is now inside your individual Phase 1 code folder and that the original top-level <strong>StampC3_Template</strong> is unchanged.</li></ol><div class="measure-row"><span><b>Copy</b> the complete template</span><span><b>Rename</b> folder and <code>.ino</code> together</span><span><b>Keep</b> the original unchanged</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-07" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">07</div>
            <div class="exercise-content"><div class="exercise-heading"><div><span class="concept-tag">Physical robot · known baseline</span><h2>Edit, Compile and Upload to the StampC3</h2></div>
<span class="time-badge">25–35 min</span></div>
<div class="teaching-brief"><p>Use the VS Code tasks to prepare support for the M5StampC3, then edit, compile, and upload the individual sketch you prepared in Exercise 6.</p>
</div>
<div class="exercise-grid"><div class="investigation"><h3>Run checklist</h3><ol><li>Run <strong>StampC3: Install M5Stack board support</strong> from VS Code's task menu.</li><li>Run <strong>StampC3: Refresh IntelliSense configuration</strong> and enter the relative path to your individual sketch, for example <code>investigations/phase-1/student-1-motors/code/Test1</code>.</li><li>Edit <code>myAP</code> to a unique access-point name and set <code>myPassword</code> to a dedicated password that you do not reuse for an account, application, or service.</li><li>Run <strong>StampC3: Compile StampC3 sketch</strong>, entering the same individual sketch path, then run <strong>StampC3: Compile and upload StampC3 sketch</strong> and enter its serial port.</li><li>Use the 115200-baud serial-monitor task or Arduino IDE fallback to record the first visible output or error.</li></ol><div class="measure-row"><span><b>Edit</b> your individual sketch</span><span><b>Compile</b> the same path</span><span><b>Record</b> the upload result</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-08" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">08</div>
            <div class="exercise-content"><div class="exercise-heading"><div><span class="concept-tag">Simulation environment · visual model</span><h2>Install and Configure Processing IDE</h2></div>
<span class="time-badge">10–20 min</span></div>
<div class="teaching-brief"><p>Processing runs the supplied Digital Twin on your computer. It is separate from VS Code, which edits and uploads the StampC3 controller.</p>
</div>
<div class="exercise-grid"><div class="investigation"><h3>Setup checklist</h3><ol><li>Install <a href="https://processing.org/download/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Processing 4</a>.</li><li>Open Processing once and confirm the editor starts in Java mode.</li><li>Open the supplied Digital Twin sketch from the <strong>DigitalTwin</strong> folder in your cloned team repository.</li></ol><div class="measure-row"><span><b>Check</b> Processing opens</span><span><b>Identify</b> Run and console</span><span><b>Keep</b> launch errors</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-09" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">09</div>
            <div class="exercise-content"><div class="exercise-heading"><div><span class="concept-tag">Common baseline · physical and simulated run</span><h2>Run the Digital Twin Demonstration</h2></div>
<span class="time-badge">30–40 min</span></div>
<div class="teaching-brief"><p>Run the supplied physical and simulated examples as parallel systems. The Digital Twin computes its own behaviour; it is not a display of robot telemetry. Observe the two systems before changing their code or constants.</p>
</div>
<div class="exercise-grid"><div class="investigation"><h3>Run and observation checklist</h3><ol><li>Open your copied StampC3 sketch in VS Code, compile it, and upload it.</li><li>Open the Processing sketch in <strong>DigitalTwin</strong> and run it.</li><li>Start the physical robot as directed and confirm both systems begin from the supplied baseline.</li><li>Describe both paths without explaining them yet.</li><li>Identify one agreement and one disagreement. State separately what you observed and what you inferred.</li><li>Choose one question that could be investigated by measuring motors, surface reflectance, or odometry on the real robot.</li><li>If either system fails, record the environment, visible symptom, and first relevant error before changing code.</li></ol><div class="measure-row"><span><b>Run</b> physical and simulated examples</span><span><b>Separate</b> observation from explanation</span><span><b>Pose</b> one testable question</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-10" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">10</div>
            <div class="exercise-content"><div class="exercise-heading"><div><span class="concept-tag">Telemetry · shared data format</span><h2>Take Measurements: Serial and Wifi</h2></div>
<span class="time-badge">20–30 min</span></div>
<div class="teaching-brief"><p>The StampC3 provides information through direct Serial and the supplied WiFi server. The transport changes, but you should always ask what a received value represents and when it was measured.</p>
</div>
<div class="exercise-grid"><div class="investigation"><h3>Measurement checklist</h3><ol><li>Use Serial to identify a timestamp and one changing value before interpreting it.</li><li>Start the supplied WiFi/TCP server and simulator, then identify one telemetry record received by the Digital Twin.</li><li>Record a field name, units or range if known, timestamp, and whether it is a command, sensor reading, encoder count, or pose estimate.</li></ol><div class="measure-row"><span><b>Serial</b> inspect a direct record</span><span><b>WiFi</b> confirm a TCP record</span><span><b>Interpret</b> field, time, and source</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
          </article>
